{"title":"2012 CEMC Euclid Problems/Problem 6a","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_6\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Euclid_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2012 CEMC Euclid Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Euclid_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=T-1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Euclid_Problems/Problem_6a&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Blaise and Pierre will play 6 games of squash. Since they are equally skilled, each is equally likely to win any given game. (In squash, there are no ties.) The probability that each of them will win 3 of the 6 games is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/0/ca06360e5e53ef1471b7c7ff1b0bd7b40c5dd88e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"38\" />. What is the probability that Blaise will win more games than Pierre?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Euclid_Problems/Problem_6a&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since the probability of both players winning the same number of games is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/0/ca06360e5e53ef1471b7c7ff1b0bd7b40c5dd88e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"38\" />, the probability of one player winning more games than the other is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/58628e41ceb63eb76d14cad32f0f1660dff46f00.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - \\frac{5}{16} = \\frac{16 - 5}{16} = \\frac{11}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"179\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>We can then realize that it is equally likely for Pierre to win more games than Blaise because they are equally skilled. Thus, the probability of Blaise winning more games than Pierre is half of this, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/2/d1260383429b753ccbf9c388851fea9ed615f182.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{11}{16} \\div 2 = \\boxed{\\frac{11}{32}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"113\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Euclid_Problems/Problem_6a&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possiblities for the winner in each game, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/0/a901d5103f4658787ce321610aa348a76ea79fb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^6 = 64$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"17\" /> possible ways for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> games to play out.\n</p><p>Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> games, one must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> wins to have more wins than the other person. Thus, we can split the problem into three different cases based on how many wins that Blaise gets. We also don't have to worry about having different probabilities for each person, as each person is equally likely to win any given game.\n</p><p>First, if Blaise gets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> wins, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways of arranging Blaise's wins, which would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Next, if Blaise gets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> wins, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways of arranging Blaise's wins, which would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Last, if Blaise gets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> wins, there would only be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> way of this happening because he would just win every game.\n</p><p>This means that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f9c0e285d9b255eef3d48ecd408ead2b589256b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15 + 6 + 1 = 22$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"15\" /> outcomes where Blaise gets more wins than Pierre.\n</p><p>Thus, the probability of Blaise winning more games than Pierre is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/1/181a3b6ba7d23ae489055e207a5d3e9285a3f5b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{22}{64} = \\boxed{\\frac{11}{32}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"82\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017230431\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.034 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 90/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 78/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4115/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    7.090      1 2012_CEMC_Euclid_Problems/Problem_6\n100.00%    7.090      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36330:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017230431 and revision id 281380. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}